1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,480
Rederive it.

2
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:03,880
Don't take it on trust.

3
00:00:05,080 --> 00:00:06,620
A paper reports an effect.

4
00:00:06,940 --> 00:00:09,571
The statistics say it is unlikely
to be chance,

5
00:00:09,571 --> 00:00:11,100
and the journal published it.

6
00:00:11,700 --> 00:00:13,164
You can trust the paper,

7
00:00:13,164 --> 00:00:16,580
or you can run the study again and see
whether the effect comes back.

8
00:00:17,180 --> 00:00:18,840
Trust is cheap and fast.

9
00:00:19,160 --> 00:00:23,340
Running it again is slow, and it is
the only one of the two that can say no.

10
00:00:24,540 --> 00:00:27,137
In 2015, the Open Science Collaboration

11
00:00:27,137 --> 00:00:30,760
repeated one hundred psychology studies
from three journals.

12
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:34,720
Ninety-seven of the hundred originals
had reported a positive result.

13
00:00:35,040 --> 00:00:36,487
Of those ninety-seven,

14
00:00:36,487 --> 00:00:39,020
thirty-five came back
significant in the repeat.

15
00:00:39,620 --> 00:00:43,600
On average, the repeated effects
were about half the size of the originals.

16
00:00:44,200 --> 00:00:46,420
That does not show
the other studies were wrong.

17
00:00:46,740 --> 00:00:49,040
A single repeat can miss a real effect.

18
00:00:49,360 --> 00:00:52,300
It shows how much a published result
can rest on one run.

19
00:00:53,500 --> 00:00:55,278
Re-running an analysis is cheaper

20
00:00:55,278 --> 00:00:58,160
than re-running a study,
if the data and code exist.

21
00:00:58,760 --> 00:01:02,607
Victoria Stodden and colleagues took
two hundred and four papers from Science,

22
00:01:02,607 --> 00:01:05,099
published after the journal
required authors to share

23
00:01:05,099 --> 00:01:07,320
data and code on request,
and asked for them.

24
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:10,894
They got the materials
for forty-four percent of the papers,

25
00:01:10,894 --> 00:01:13,760
and estimated that about
twenty-six percent would reproduce.

26
00:01:14,360 --> 00:01:16,927
A rule that relies
on the author answering an email

27
00:01:16,927 --> 00:01:18,760
checks only the authors who answer.

28
00:01:19,960 --> 00:01:22,330
Web certificates are public records,

29
00:01:22,330 --> 00:01:25,160
so anyone can check them
without asking the issuer.

30
00:01:25,760 --> 00:01:29,383
Deepak Kumar and colleagues wrote
two hundred and twenty checks and ran them

31
00:01:29,383 --> 00:01:31,760
over two hundred
and forty million certificates.

32
00:01:32,360 --> 00:01:36,180
In 2012, more than twelve percent
of certificates had errors.

33
00:01:36,500 --> 00:01:39,960
By 2017 the share
was two hundredths of one percent.

34
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:43,380
The paper does not show
the checks caused that fall.

35
00:01:43,700 --> 00:01:45,697
It shows a public record can be re-checked

36
00:01:45,697 --> 00:01:47,980
by a stranger,
at the scale of the whole web.

37
00:01:49,180 --> 00:01:52,100
A result you can re-derive
does not need your trust.

38
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:55,740
Ask for the data,
the code and a check you can run yourself.

39
00:01:56,340 --> 00:01:58,660
The voice you heard
is a synthesized version of mine.

40
00:01:58,980 --> 00:02:01,840
Every number on screen links
to its source under the video.
